# Every coupling, beside the fusion that swallowed it if one did. One row per filed coupling,
# with the composed layer holding both of its ends where such a layer exists.
#
# A referral, not a violation
#   That distinction keeps this file honest. Detecting the structure is mechanical: two coupled
#   layers, one composed layer, both ends inside it. Judging whether the composer should have said
#   so is prose, and prose is a person's job. So these are handed over rather than passed or
#   failed, and a clean run has rows here.
#
# Why it matters
#   Once two coupled layers are inside one total, their dependence stops being visible from
#   outside. Whoever reads the composed figure cannot tell that part of it moves together, and
#   that is exactly the information a fusion is supposed to preserve. The schema asks the composer
#   to say so; nothing can make them.
#
# The unabsorbed rows are the denominator
#   They are here on purpose. A referral that printed only its findings would be as unreadable as
#   a rule that printed only its violations: five couplings examined and two absorbed is a
#   statement; two rows is not.
#
# Corpus only
#   Every report in this directory owes saying so. A referral hands a document to a person to
#   judge, and a fixture's prose is stipulated rather than authored: there is nobody to refer it
#   to and nothing for them to weigh. No fixture files a coupling today, so this join changes no
#   row; that is exactly the moment to write it, rather than the run after the first one does.
-- pm:Coupling against asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part pairs that contain both of its ends, over the corpus.
SELECT c.filing,
       c.from_layer || ' -> ' || c.to_layer AS coupling,
       CASE WHEN a.composition IS NULL THEN '(no fusion holds both ends)'
            ELSE a.composition || '/' || a.composed_layer END AS absorbed_into,
       left(regexp_replace(c.observation, '\s+', ' ', 'g'), 56) || '...' AS what_was_observed
FROM (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT DISTINCT sp.part_filing AS filing, sp.from_layer, sp.to_layer,
           sp.composition, sp.composed_layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/sibling_parts.sqlc)
    ) sp
) a USING (filing, from_layer, to_layer)
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) sc USING (filing)
ORDER BY 1, 2
